A JOIN in SQL is used to combine rows from two or more tables based on a related column between them. Joins allow you to retrieve meaningful data spread across multiple tables.

Types of Joins
INNER JOIN

Returns rows where there is a match in both tables.

SELECT users.id, users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
INNER JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
LEFT JOIN (LEFT OUTER JOIN)

Returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table. If no match, returns NULL for the right table's columns.

SELECT users.id, users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
LEFT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
RIGHT JOIN (RIGHT OUTER JOIN)

Returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table. If no match, returns NULL for the left table's columns.

SELECT users.id, users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
RIGHT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
FULL JOIN (FULL OUTER JOIN)

Returns all rows from both tables. Rows without matches in either table are filled with NULL.
Note: Not directly supported in MySQL. Can be simulated with UNION:

SELECT users.id, users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
LEFT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id
UNION
SELECT users.id, users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
RIGHT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
CROSS JOIN

Produces a Cartesian product of the two tables. Every row in the first table is combined with every row in the second table.

SELECT users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
CROSS JOIN orders;
SELF JOIN

Joins a table with itself. Often used to find hierarchical relationships.

SELECT e1.name AS Employee, e2.name AS Manager
FROM employees e1
INNER JOIN employees e2 ON e1.manager_id = e2.id;



Join Example with Sample Tables
Table: users
id	name
1	John
2	Jane
3	Alice
Table: orders
id	user_id	amount
1	1	100.00
2	2	150.00
Queries:
INNER JOIN

SELECT users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
INNER JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
Result:

name	amount
John	100.00
Jane	150.00
LEFT JOIN

SELECT users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
LEFT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
Result:

name	amount
John	100.00
Jane	150.00
Alice	NULL
RIGHT JOIN

SELECT users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
RIGHT JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id;
Result:

name	amount
John	100.00
Jane	150.00
CROSS JOIN

SELECT users.name, orders.amount
FROM users
CROSS JOIN orders;
Result:

name	amount
John	100.00
John	150.00
Jane	100.00
Jane	150.00
Alice	100.00
Alice	150.00



Best Practices for Using Joins
Index Foreign Keys: Improve performance by indexing the columns used in JOIN conditions.
Filter Early: Use WHERE clauses to limit the data being joined.
Use Aliases: Simplify query readability with table aliases.

SELECT u.name, o.amount
FROM users AS u
INNER JOIN orders AS o ON u.id = o.user_id;
